00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:24.400
FRANCESC CAMPOY: This
weekend I watched

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:25.890
a bunch of Cloud Next docs.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:29.020
I always said I was
binge watching Next.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:32.050
And one of the talks,
actually two of the talks

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:38.800
that really blew my mind
were from Martin Gorner

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:39.760
from our team.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:41.920
He gave two talks on
TensorFlow, they're

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:45.040
called "TensorFlow and Deep
Learning without a PhD."

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:47.770
And it turned out very well
because I do not have a PhD.

00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:51.700
And he basically goes from what
are the basics of TensorFlow

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:56.380
to training a neural network
with all the works of William

00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:01.335
Shakespeare, and generating
new William Shakespeare fan

00:02:01.335 --> 00:02:04.780
fiction, you could say.

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:08.350
William Shakespeare fan fiction
with [? Daniel ?] network--

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:10.090
and the result is amazing.

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:14.020
He also uses the same technique
to generate Python code.

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:17.290
So he trains the
TensorFlow neural network

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:20.260
with the TensorFlow
source code, and then he

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:22.235
generates new TensorFlow code.

00:02:22.235 --> 00:02:24.360
MARK MANDEL: It looks like
it would work in theory.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:25.443
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, yeah!

00:02:25.443 --> 00:02:26.830
MARK MANDEL: Like, close.

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:29.330
FRANCESC CAMPOY: To the point
that it starts with the Apache

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:32.350
v2 license on top of the file.

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:33.770
It is actually really amazing.

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:38.400
It generates code that looks
like I could have written it,

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.240
really.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:43.731
Also, I don't write
that good Python.

00:02:43.731 --> 00:02:44.950
I guess that's why.

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:47.120
MARK MANDEL: We
probably can't judge.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:49.160
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
But go check it out.

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:51.880
It's like two hours of
content, but it's definitely

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:54.280
worth your time if you
are curious about what

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.500
you can do with deep
learning, and you

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:58.540
want to learn from scratch
and without getting

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:01.330
too much detail into the
mathematics concept behind it--

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:03.090
you just want to
know how to do it.

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:04.510
MARK MANDEL: Cool!

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:07.600
My cool thing is the
announcement of the Data Loss

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:09.484
Prevention API.

00:03:09.484 --> 00:03:10.900
I actually think
it's really cool.

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:13.250
When I first actually saw
this, I just saw the video

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:15.140
and I was like, oh,
that's really neat.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:16.810
And then I realized it was
our product, in which case

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:18.190
I was actually really excited.

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:20.710
So the cool thing about
this is it's, again,

00:03:20.710 --> 00:03:22.810
a predefined machine
learning tool.

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:25.750
But what it does is it
will remove and classify

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:28.660
sensitive information, so social
security numbers, credit card

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:30.880
numbers, phone numbers,
all sorts of other types

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:31.631
of sensitive data.

00:03:31.631 --> 00:03:34.171
And you don't really have to do
a huge amount of work for it.

00:03:34.171 --> 00:03:35.656
So you can just
send it at the API

00:03:35.656 --> 00:03:37.780
and then it will send it
back going, oh, hey, let's

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:40.280
just redact all these things
from it, which is pretty sweet.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:41.470
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:45.210
I discovered this thing during
one of the Cloud Next keynotes.

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:47.770
It was really cool
because they show,

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:50.560
imagine that you're in a chat
and you're talking to someone

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:52.930
and you're like, hey, I will
need your, I don't know,

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:54.117
your account number.

00:03:54.117 --> 00:03:56.200
And they're like, yeah,
this is my account number,

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:59.500
this is my social security
number, this is my address,

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:01.000
and this is how to
find my children.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.820
You know, it's like,
maybe not give me that,

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:06.340
but now it's already in the
chat so you're storing it.

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:08.690
And with this API,
basically you're

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:12.280
able to find all the
things that are sensitive

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:13.300
and filter them out.

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:16.779
So you can now give access
only to the people that should

00:04:16.779 --> 00:04:18.579
be having access to these.

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:21.122
The amazing thing is that
also works on images.

00:04:21.122 --> 00:04:22.330
I don't know if you saw that.

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:23.290
MARK MANDEL: Oh,
I didn't see that.

00:04:23.290 --> 00:04:24.910
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
they have an example where

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:26.440
they put a picture,
and the picture

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:28.510
is a picture of a credit card.

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:31.330
And on the credit
card, it removes only

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:34.427
the credit card number, but
not the name of the person.

00:04:34.427 --> 00:04:37.010
Because the name of the person
is not considered as sensitive,

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:39.130
but the credit card number is.

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:40.386
So it is really cool.

00:04:40.386 --> 00:04:42.010
MARK MANDEL: It is
really, really cool.

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:43.030
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
it's pretty amazing.

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:44.140
I guess that that's
one of the things

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:46.480
that you can do when you
have amazing deep learning

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:47.317
with TensorFlow.

00:04:47.317 --> 00:04:48.400
MARK MANDEL: Yes, exactly.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:49.690
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Looking
