00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:46.941
why don't you tell us a
little bit about yourself

00:06:46.941 --> 00:06:49.220
and what do you do
at Server Density.

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:50.360
DAVID MYTTON: Yeah, sure.

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:53.700
I'm the co-founder and
CEO at Server Density.

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:57.340
We are a software-as-a-service
based scalable infrastructure

00:06:57.340 --> 00:06:58.760
monitoring company.

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:01.790
We help our customers
save time and money

00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:05.550
by providing advanced server
and website monitoring, sites

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:07.820
alerting, waking people
up in the middle of night

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:11.060
when stuff's going wrong,
fancy graphs and integrations

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:12.940
across all the cloud providers.

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:15.290
And we've got some
customers like the NHS,

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:17.420
with the emergency
response service

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:21.056
and ambulances here in the
UK, and also Drupal, Firebox,

00:07:21.056 --> 00:07:22.722
and [? especially ?]
the graphing is all

00:07:22.722 --> 00:07:24.600
we're going to be
talking about in a bit.

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:25.440
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:28.230
And what do you do
at Server Density?

00:07:28.230 --> 00:07:31.220
DAVID MYTTON: I started
the company back in 2009.

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:34.060
So at that point I
was doing everything.

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:36.350
I am along with
my co-founder who

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:38.312
I was working on the
design and the interface,

00:07:38.312 --> 00:07:39.770
and we put a lot
of time and effort

00:07:39.770 --> 00:07:43.320
into that, building and
graphing the interface.

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:45.480
But I built the original
version of the product,

00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:49.070
including our original
time series database.

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:51.140
And over the years
I've hired engineers

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:55.310
who are actually qualified and
have rewritten most of the code

00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:57.560
that I wrote in the old days.

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:01.910
So now I'm responsible for
the overall company vision,

00:08:01.910 --> 00:08:03.410
how we execute on things.

00:08:03.410 --> 00:08:06.385
I am working on the commercial
side as well as dipping in here

00:08:06.385 --> 00:08:07.510
and now in the engineering.

00:08:07.510 --> 00:08:07.960
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:09.620
So could you tell
us a little bit

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:13.670
about what is
Server Density like?

00:08:13.670 --> 00:08:15.140
How it is to use it?

00:08:15.140 --> 00:08:17.720
Do you have an API
and people just call

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:18.940
for every single log line?

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:20.270
Do you open a TCP connection?

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:24.445
How is the developer
experience with Server Density?

00:08:24.445 --> 00:08:26.820
DAVID MYTTON: There's two
different parts to the product.

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:30.170
The first one, and the original
use case, is server monitoring,

00:08:30.170 --> 00:08:34.089
and then that's complemented
by availability monitoring.

00:08:34.089 --> 00:08:36.005
And with the server
monitoring side of things,

00:08:36.005 --> 00:08:38.150
there's an open source agent.

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:40.400
So I wrote the original
version of that.

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:44.120
And it's packaged up, and you
install it onto your servers.

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:48.770
It runs, executes, commands to
polling the system statistics,

00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:51.242
and ties in with
all the applications

00:08:51.242 --> 00:08:52.700
you might be running
on the server,

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:55.940
so databases, web servers,
that kind of thing.

00:08:55.940 --> 00:08:58.970
It then creates a
payload in JSON,

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:01.460
sends the data back
to our server HTTPS.

00:09:01.460 --> 00:09:05.930
And that comes into the
Server Density Platform.

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:09.620
From there, it runs
through an alerting engine.

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:12.500
And that's where we will decide
if the alerts that you've

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:14.210
configured as a
customer are going

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:16.690
to be triggered, whether
we're going to email you, SMS

00:09:16.690 --> 00:09:22.160
you, send messages to Slack or
HipChat or anything like that.

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:26.120
And then it routes it to the
time series storage engine

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:29.830
so that we can store that
data for historical reporting,

00:09:29.830 --> 00:09:32.360
building graphs, and
showing up dashboards.

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:35.090
And then the second part is
the availability monitoring.

00:09:35.090 --> 00:09:37.770
And we have locations
around the world.

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:41.951
That's where we will poll you
APIs and public applications,

00:09:41.951 --> 00:09:42.450
endpoints.

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:44.575
Anything that's
available online,

00:09:44.575 --> 00:09:45.950
we're looking at
the availability

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:47.190
and the performance.

00:09:47.190 --> 00:09:48.710
And then everything
is accessible

00:09:48.710 --> 00:09:51.080
through our mobile
apps, our web interface,

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:52.654
and also through our API.

00:09:52.654 --> 00:09:53.570
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:09:53.570 --> 00:09:55.790
So one of the things
that surprised me out

00:09:55.790 --> 00:09:58.160
of the whole thing
that you said was

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:02.310
to log stuff, your open
source agent, what it does

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:07.270
is it sends JSON
payload over HTTP.

00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:08.980
And I'm wondering, why is that?

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:11.140
Why not something like gRPC.

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:12.650
Other than the fact
that, of course,

00:10:12.650 --> 00:10:15.500
when you created the agent,
gRPC was not a thing.

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:20.110
But like any other option,
binaries or whatever.

00:10:20.110 --> 00:10:21.860
DAVID MYTTON: There
were two main reasons.

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:26.680
The first one is that HTTP is
a very well known protocol.

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:30.670
And outbound HTTPS, because
we encrypt the payload,

00:10:30.670 --> 00:10:32.965
is typically available
on most environments.

00:10:32.965 --> 00:10:35.410
Or if it's not directly
available, then

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:36.640
often there's a proxy.

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:39.310
And avoiding a
custom protocol means

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:43.330
that it's very easy to convince
people to open firewall rules

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:46.360
if they need to for
outbound connectivity.

00:10:46.360 --> 00:10:50.200
And the second reason
is, in the early days,

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:52.510
I didn't really know
much about scaling,

00:10:52.510 --> 00:10:54.940
I had scaling services,
scaling web services,

00:10:54.940 --> 00:10:58.030
but I didn't know about
how to set up load balances

00:10:58.030 --> 00:10:59.210
and to scale web.

00:10:59.210 --> 00:11:01.180
And I thought that
it would be easier

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:05.050
to scale something just
by using load balances

00:11:05.050 --> 00:11:09.160
and Apache at the time, now
NGINX, and then inventing

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:10.222
a custom protocol.

00:11:10.222 --> 00:11:12.430
MARK MANDEL: So you talked
a little bit about before,

00:11:12.430 --> 00:11:15.170
you said you set up the
original implementation

00:11:15.170 --> 00:11:16.560
of how Server Density works.

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:18.310
Can you talk us a
little bit through that?

00:11:18.310 --> 00:11:20.080
I believe you weren't
running on Google Cloud,

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:21.740
you were doing some
other different things?

00:11:21.740 --> 00:11:23.073
DAVID MYTTON: Yes, that's right.

00:11:23.073 --> 00:11:25.240
So there's a couple
of different steps.

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:29.605
And we have migrated a large
portion of the application

00:11:29.605 --> 00:11:31.390
over to Google Cloud,
but there's still

00:11:31.390 --> 00:11:33.520
a few bits in our
older infrastructure

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:36.790
that's gradually being moved
over over the next few months.

00:11:36.790 --> 00:11:39.180
But all I can talk about
is the how it was before,

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:40.930
and then we can go
into all the cool stuff

00:11:40.930 --> 00:11:42.812
on Google Cloud
about how it is now.

00:11:42.812 --> 00:11:44.020
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Sounds good.

00:11:44.020 --> 00:11:47.632
DAVID MYTTON: So when
the payload comes in,

00:11:47.632 --> 00:11:49.090
what came in through
our system, it

00:11:49.090 --> 00:11:51.860
goes into the intake
part of the application.

00:11:51.860 --> 00:11:55.840
So this is just an
endpoint, an HTTPS endpoint.

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:58.860
And the data comes
in, and that is then

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:01.000
passed through our
load balancers, which

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:03.280
were Apache in
the original days,

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:06.010
and are now running off NGINX.

00:12:06.010 --> 00:12:08.470
And that is inserted into
a queuing system, which

00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:11.980
we built on top of MongoDB.

00:12:11.980 --> 00:12:14.950
The data is stored there,
and then very quickly

00:12:14.950 --> 00:12:17.230
is pulled out by
the rules engine.

00:12:17.230 --> 00:12:19.075
The original version
of that was in PHP.

00:12:19.075 --> 00:12:21.850
That was the first
language that I learnt.

00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:25.120
And it has a bad
reputation, but is

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:27.520
incredibly versatile in
being able to do stuff really

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:28.790
quickly.

00:12:28.790 --> 00:12:31.635
So the rules engine
looks at the payload.

00:12:31.635 --> 00:12:33.760
It does a quick check to
make sure it's valid JSON.

00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:35.210
The account exists.

00:12:35.210 --> 00:12:37.570
The account is fully
paid up and is active.

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:41.290
And then it will pass
through all the data

00:12:41.290 --> 00:12:45.040
and compare it to the
alerts that are configured.

00:12:45.040 --> 00:12:46.780
And we do a number
of different checks

00:12:46.780 --> 00:12:48.740
to see if an alert
is already open.

00:12:48.740 --> 00:12:51.100
If it is already open,
then we might not

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:53.830
want to send another
notification immediately.

00:12:53.830 --> 00:12:55.630
And there are
different rules that

00:12:55.630 --> 00:12:58.300
allow us to send notifications
at different times.

00:12:58.300 --> 00:13:00.410
Once the letter is
being opened, we're

00:13:00.410 --> 00:13:03.480
waiting for a certain period
of time for an alert condition

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:07.040
to exist for a couple of minutes
before we send notifications.

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:10.690
But it's all based on
customer configuration.

00:13:10.690 --> 00:13:13.240
If we decide to
send a notification,

00:13:13.240 --> 00:13:15.350
then there's a
separate queue, again,

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:20.200
in some MongoDB-based queue,
and our notification service,

00:13:20.200 --> 00:13:22.750
which has been
rewritten into Python,

00:13:22.750 --> 00:13:25.870
and that was the second
language that I learnt.

00:13:25.870 --> 00:13:29.740
And that is what sends
emails, sends SMSes,

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:32.200
ties into push
notification gateways,

00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:34.660
and sends that out
to the customer.

00:13:34.660 --> 00:13:37.510
And then there's a
second route, which

00:13:37.510 --> 00:13:41.480
goes into MongoDB for the
time series storage as well.

00:13:41.480 --> 00:13:44.040
So that's for the graphing
and the recording.

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:46.605
So there are these two
channels, one for alerting,

00:13:46.605 --> 00:13:49.666
and one goes through the rules
engine and then we discard it.

00:13:49.666 --> 00:13:51.040
And then there's
a second channel

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:53.290
where it goes into the
time series format,

00:13:53.290 --> 00:13:56.560
is transformed into an
optimized storage format,

00:13:56.560 --> 00:13:58.754
and then goes into MongoDB.

00:13:58.754 --> 00:14:00.920
MARK MANDEL: So this was
the previous implementation

00:14:00.920 --> 00:14:02.794
that was running all
these MongoDB instances.

00:14:02.794 --> 00:14:03.657
Is that right?

00:14:03.657 --> 00:14:04.990
DAVID MYTTON: Yes, that's right.

00:14:04.990 --> 00:14:09.250
And that was running on bare
metal servers at SoftLayer.

00:14:09.250 --> 00:14:13.690
And we had hundreds of gigabytes
of RAM, many terabytes of SSDs.

00:14:13.690 --> 00:14:17.225
And it was several years
worth of iterations on that

00:14:17.225 --> 00:14:21.070
that I did in the very
beginning to give us really,

00:14:21.070 --> 00:14:24.490
really fast loading graphs
for what is the most commonly

00:14:24.490 --> 00:14:26.380
accessed time view,
which is usually

00:14:26.380 --> 00:14:29.020
the last couple of hours
up to the last 24 hours

00:14:29.020 --> 00:14:32.110
for customers, and that
is slightly less optimized

00:14:32.110 --> 00:14:35.500
in terms of the graph
loading time for time periods

00:14:35.500 --> 00:14:36.592
going beyond that.

00:14:36.592 --> 00:14:37.800
MARK MANDEL: I'm curious now.

00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:40.760
So you're running like your own
sort of sharded MongoDB replica

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:41.530
sets?

00:14:41.530 --> 00:14:42.982
How are you managing that?

00:14:42.982 --> 00:14:44.440
Was that all just
a manual process?

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:46.110
How was that managed?

00:14:46.110 --> 00:14:48.470
DAVID MYTTON: Yeah, it was
the MongoDB replica sets

00:14:48.470 --> 00:14:49.750
way of sharding.

00:14:49.750 --> 00:14:52.780
So that was a single database
and a single collection

00:14:52.780 --> 00:14:58.880
in MongoDB, and we split it out
into different granularities.

00:14:58.880 --> 00:15:01.720
So the agent reports
back every minute

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:05.340
and we store that data
every minute for a week.

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:06.900
And then it's also,
at the same time,

00:15:06.900 --> 00:15:08.275
written into a
different database

00:15:08.275 --> 00:15:10.380
to give hourly averages.

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:12.330
And that data is stored forever.

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:14.400
And due to the
architecture of MongoDB

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:18.240
at the time, the database
level lock that they had,

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:20.640
which has now been improved
significantly in the latest

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:23.580
storage engines, we split them
out into different databases

00:15:23.580 --> 00:15:25.440
and sharded them separately.

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:28.980
But aside from that being
kind of a limitation of Mongo,

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:32.610
it also allowed us the benefit
of being able to split them out

00:15:32.610 --> 00:15:35.910
into different clusters
based on the use case,

00:15:35.910 --> 00:15:40.950
because hourly data is accessed
less often, so we could have

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:43.314
cheaper hardware to
power that cluster.

00:15:43.314 --> 00:15:44.230
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:15:44.230 --> 00:15:47.460
So you were mentioning that
that is previous architecture.

00:15:47.460 --> 00:15:49.080
So what about the new one?

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:51.300
What were the
issues, or what were

00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:53.460
the advantages on the
new architecture that

00:15:53.460 --> 00:15:55.410
made you move?

00:15:55.410 --> 00:15:57.600
DAVID MYTTON: There were
a couple of challenges.

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:01.680
And we reached these,
I think, about a year

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:03.330
ago to the beginning
of last year.

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:05.820
And we got to the point
where the current system

00:16:05.820 --> 00:16:07.527
was no longer scaling.

00:16:07.527 --> 00:16:09.360
In particular, we had
to spend a lot of time

00:16:09.360 --> 00:16:11.250
maintaining that infrastructure.

00:16:11.250 --> 00:16:13.320
So we didn't own the
servers, but they

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:14.760
were bare metal servers.

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:16.500
And we were renting
them from SoftLayer.

00:16:16.500 --> 00:16:18.840
And every so often we'd have
disk failures or hardware

00:16:18.840 --> 00:16:22.620
failures and we'd have to
deal with upgrading them.

00:16:22.620 --> 00:16:25.350
So there was just a lot of
time consuming maintenance

00:16:25.350 --> 00:16:27.570
that our team had to do.

00:16:27.570 --> 00:16:30.180
We also wanted to build
a lot more features.

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:34.200
And because we'd completely
custom-designed essentially

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:38.250
the database schema to fit
this, and the APIs, all

00:16:38.250 --> 00:16:42.180
the functionality, and the
whole architecture around it,

00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:43.860
it was a completely
custom system.

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:46.860
And we're a small team, we're
20 people, which two of them

00:16:46.860 --> 00:16:50.620
are engineers, but we have so
many feature requests coming in

00:16:50.620 --> 00:16:54.870
that we didn't have enough
time to dedicate to building.

00:16:54.870 --> 00:16:58.020
I was spending a lot of time
building time series, data

00:16:58.020 --> 00:17:01.410
functionality, so things
like doing math functions

00:17:01.410 --> 00:17:04.920
and being able to pull out data
from different granularities

00:17:04.920 --> 00:17:05.609
and combine it.

00:17:05.609 --> 00:17:07.560
All the applications,
all of these standard

00:17:07.560 --> 00:17:09.510
features that you'd
expect to be able to see,

00:17:09.510 --> 00:17:12.560
we didn't have
time to build them.

00:17:12.560 --> 00:17:15.390
Also, the scaling
was a challenge.

00:17:15.390 --> 00:17:19.200
So the replication and scaling
in MongoDB is pretty good.

00:17:19.200 --> 00:17:21.960
But you can't do it
in a linear fashion.

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:25.950
You have to set up an entirely
new cluster for a new shard.

00:17:25.950 --> 00:17:28.629
And then data has to
move around, to migrate.

00:17:28.629 --> 00:17:30.420
You've got to make sure
that there's enough

00:17:30.420 --> 00:17:34.020
overhead to move that data.

00:17:34.020 --> 00:17:38.160
So adding new clusters
in was an overhead.

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:39.990
And then, finally,
it's very expensive

00:17:39.990 --> 00:17:43.380
to buy all that memory
and all those SSDs.

00:17:43.380 --> 00:17:45.330
And it was a fun thing
to say that we've

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:48.300
got terabytes of SSDs,
but when you're actually

00:17:48.300 --> 00:17:50.942
looking at the overall cost,
it was really expensive.

00:17:50.942 --> 00:17:51.900
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:17:51.900 --> 00:17:54.440
So what does the new
architecture look like?

00:17:54.440 --> 00:17:55.960
I think you're on GCP now.

00:17:55.960 --> 00:17:57.210
What are you using?

00:17:57.210 --> 00:17:58.400
What's connected to what?

00:17:58.400 --> 00:17:59.790
DAVID MYTTON:
Yeah, that's right.

00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:03.360
So the time series
database that was MongoDB

00:18:03.360 --> 00:18:05.580
is now Cloud Bigtable.

00:18:05.580 --> 00:18:08.850
And that is running
mostly on Google Cloud.

00:18:08.850 --> 00:18:11.970
But the intake aspect of
it is still in SoftLayer.

00:18:11.970 --> 00:18:13.920
It's just in a new architecture.

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:17.160
And it was about 18
months ago, two years ago,

00:18:17.160 --> 00:18:18.330
that we rewrote that bit.

00:18:18.330 --> 00:18:22.420
And unfortunately the Pub/Sub
product wasn't fully available.

00:18:22.420 --> 00:18:24.720
So we ended up having
to build it ourselves

00:18:24.720 --> 00:18:26.880
on top of Kafka install.

00:18:26.880 --> 00:18:30.231
So to go back to how
the data comes in--

00:18:30.231 --> 00:18:32.730
it's coming in from the agent,
it goes into that intake part

00:18:32.730 --> 00:18:33.430
again.

00:18:33.430 --> 00:18:36.000
So instead of it going
into a MongoDB queue,

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:38.280
it now goes into Kafka.

00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:43.800
So Kafka is the Apache open
source project queuing system.

00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:48.420
That's then pulled out using
Storm, which is a big data

00:18:48.420 --> 00:18:50.520
event-based processing system.

00:18:50.520 --> 00:18:51.900
It basically gives
us a framework

00:18:51.900 --> 00:18:54.140
to process all that data.

00:18:54.140 --> 00:18:57.480
The rules engine was rewritten
from PHP into Python.

00:18:57.480 --> 00:18:59.340
That's the language
that we've standardized

00:18:59.340 --> 00:19:00.690
across the whole company.

00:19:00.690 --> 00:19:03.990
And new things are
written in Python now.

00:19:03.990 --> 00:19:06.540
So all the rules
engine, which replicated

00:19:06.540 --> 00:19:10.230
in Python and with a modern
test suite and integration test

00:19:10.230 --> 00:19:13.110
and everything that you'd
expect from a properly planned

00:19:13.110 --> 00:19:15.730
and written project, rather
than something that I wrote

00:19:15.730 --> 00:19:17.380
and then scaled over the years.

00:19:17.380 --> 00:19:19.800
And then the notification
system, again, that first route

00:19:19.800 --> 00:19:21.170
is still the same--

00:19:21.170 --> 00:19:25.110
with some improvements,
but that's in Python still.

00:19:25.110 --> 00:19:28.350
And then the second route
is the entirely new bit

00:19:28.350 --> 00:19:30.540
on top of Google Cloud Platform.

00:19:30.540 --> 00:19:33.180
So part of this was
also-- it's also

00:19:33.180 --> 00:19:35.450
the first bit of our migration
over to Google Cloud.

00:19:35.450 --> 00:19:39.200
So we had the challenge of
running the primary structure

00:19:39.200 --> 00:19:43.770
in SoftLayer, but having the
time series database running

00:19:43.770 --> 00:19:45.570
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:19:45.570 --> 00:19:48.510
And what we have to do
is-- because the system

00:19:48.510 --> 00:19:51.180
is built in a
microservices architecture,

00:19:51.180 --> 00:19:53.250
then we have all
these APIs internally.

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:54.960
So it is relatively
straightforward

00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:58.770
to extract this entire
component, move over to Google,

00:19:58.770 --> 00:20:02.190
and have it continue to
communicate over HTTPS, even

00:20:02.190 --> 00:20:04.560
across the internet,
which is what we did.

00:20:04.560 --> 00:20:09.470
So that second route now looks
like just an internal API

00:20:09.470 --> 00:20:11.040
as opposed to HTTPS.

00:20:11.040 --> 00:20:12.840
And that goes into
the Google load

00:20:12.840 --> 00:20:15.120
balances that gives us
the high availability

00:20:15.120 --> 00:20:17.040
and the scalability.

00:20:17.040 --> 00:20:19.650
The internal API is
written in Python again.

00:20:19.650 --> 00:20:23.120
And it's just exposing
the functionality.

00:20:23.120 --> 00:20:26.610
It's a very light wrapper
on top of OpenTSDB,

00:20:26.610 --> 00:20:30.120
which is the time series
database product that we use.

00:20:30.120 --> 00:20:32.370
This is open source,
and has allowed

00:20:32.370 --> 00:20:34.770
us to solve that problem
of not having enough time

00:20:34.770 --> 00:20:38.670
to build all the features that
we want into the time series

00:20:38.670 --> 00:20:41.914
database because it has
so many different features

00:20:41.914 --> 00:20:42.955
available out of the box.

00:20:42.955 --> 00:20:45.312
So we're now starting to
build into the product.

00:20:45.312 --> 00:20:47.520
And we've been able to
contribute a few fixes back as

00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:49.920
well, because it's an
open source project.

00:20:49.920 --> 00:20:52.550
And then the final part
of it is that OpenTSDB

00:20:52.550 --> 00:20:54.690
is talking to Cloud Bigtable.

00:20:54.690 --> 00:20:58.160
And that's where the actual
storage of the data sits.

00:20:58.160 --> 00:20:59.085
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:20:59.085 --> 00:21:01.950
Tell us a little bit
about how OpenTSDB works

00:21:01.950 --> 00:21:04.140
and how that whole
thing goes together,

00:21:04.140 --> 00:21:05.730
I'm quite curious about it.

00:21:05.730 --> 00:21:07.050
DAVID MYTTON: Yes.

00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:09.750
The OpenTSDB instance
is entirely stateless,

00:21:09.750 --> 00:21:12.540
so we're running backs on
Google Container Engine,

00:21:12.540 --> 00:21:15.210
so it's all shiny new
containers running

00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:16.890
on Kubernetes, which
was a lot of fun

00:21:16.890 --> 00:21:19.260
too for our team to
play around with.

00:21:19.260 --> 00:21:23.820
But essentially, it gives you
an optimized storage format,

00:21:23.820 --> 00:21:29.520
which has a driver too, and
store that into Cloud BigTable.

00:21:29.520 --> 00:21:33.150
And it then gives you an API
to be able to query the data,

00:21:33.150 --> 00:21:37.020
and has various functions so you
can aggregate and perform math

00:21:37.020 --> 00:21:39.340
functions on top of the data--

00:21:39.340 --> 00:21:43.050
so queried based on tags.

00:21:43.050 --> 00:21:45.120
So this gives us
all those features

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:48.030
that we wanted in terms
of being able to offer

00:21:48.030 --> 00:21:49.760
the graphing to our customers.

00:21:49.760 --> 00:21:51.360
MARK MANDEL: And how
is the experience

00:21:51.360 --> 00:21:53.440
being in terms of being
able to scale BigTable?

00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:54.811
How's that worked for you?

00:21:54.811 --> 00:21:56.310
DAVID MYTTON: So
the best part of it

00:21:56.310 --> 00:21:58.980
probably is that it's a
completely managed service,

00:21:58.980 --> 00:22:02.920
and this was one of the biggest
reasons for us moving over.

00:22:02.920 --> 00:22:06.340
So when I wrote the original
version of our MongoDB system,

00:22:06.340 --> 00:22:09.060
I chose MongoDB because there
was nothing else available.

00:22:09.060 --> 00:22:12.810
BigTable didn't exist as a
publicly available product,

00:22:12.810 --> 00:22:15.450
and databases like
Cassandra, which

00:22:15.450 --> 00:22:18.270
have a good reputation
for time series data,

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:22.500
didn't exist the first time
I came to work with MongoDB.

00:22:22.500 --> 00:22:24.660
So it gave us a
really good product

00:22:24.660 --> 00:22:28.550
that scaled over the years
to have really, really fast

00:22:28.550 --> 00:22:29.100
writes.

00:22:29.100 --> 00:22:33.270
And I'm surprised at how
well it scaled over, well,

00:22:33.270 --> 00:22:35.730
seven years, really, of
the company existing.

00:22:35.730 --> 00:22:38.160
But it required a huge
amount of my time.

00:22:38.160 --> 00:22:42.030
And then, when we hired people,
this required a lot of time

00:22:42.030 --> 00:22:44.160
internally to continue
to maintain it.

00:22:44.160 --> 00:22:47.550
So just being able to have
a database product that's

00:22:47.550 --> 00:22:51.810
managed by Google, all the
failover, the redundancy,

00:22:51.810 --> 00:22:53.970
the design, everything
is just handled by Google

00:22:53.970 --> 00:22:56.640
so that we don't have to
deal with any of that.

00:22:56.640 --> 00:22:58.740
And it's not a general
purpose database.

00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:02.160
It's very specifically
designed for time series data

00:23:02.160 --> 00:23:05.040
and that means we get
incredible performance from it,

00:23:05.040 --> 00:23:06.810
and that allows us
to reduce the costs

00:23:06.810 --> 00:23:09.420
just because we don't need
all that expensive hardware.

00:23:09.420 --> 00:23:12.170
And then, specifically in
terms of the scalability,

00:23:12.170 --> 00:23:13.440
it is essentially linear.

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:18.030
We know that we can get 10,000
writes per second per node.

00:23:18.030 --> 00:23:21.090
And we just press a button,
and in a couple of minutes

00:23:21.090 --> 00:23:22.200
we have a new node.

00:23:22.200 --> 00:23:23.760
And within about
20 to 30 minutes

00:23:23.760 --> 00:23:26.700
the data has rebalanced
across the entire cluster,

00:23:26.700 --> 00:23:30.932
and we've added an
extra 10,000 writes.

00:23:30.932 --> 00:23:33.390
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Could you
share a little bit of the amount

00:23:33.390 --> 00:23:35.130
of traffic that you're doing?

00:23:35.130 --> 00:23:38.460
Or what's the difference in
cost between running your own

00:23:38.460 --> 00:23:42.150
servers-- you were saying that
you were owning so many SSDs--

00:23:42.150 --> 00:23:47.570
to just migrating everything
to Cloud BigTable?

00:23:47.570 --> 00:23:49.500
DAVID MYTTON: The
cost of Cloud BigTable

00:23:49.500 --> 00:23:52.020
is a lot more
predictable because we

00:23:52.020 --> 00:23:55.110
can see exactly how
many writes per second

00:23:55.110 --> 00:23:58.380
we're buying for
the per node cost.

00:23:58.380 --> 00:24:02.820
And the graphing the Google
Cloud Platform Control Panel

00:24:02.820 --> 00:24:05.130
provides is pretty
good and that just

00:24:05.130 --> 00:24:07.570
shows us the absolute numbers.

00:24:07.570 --> 00:24:10.509
That means we can
see pretty easily

00:24:10.509 --> 00:24:12.300
the exact number of
writes that we're doing

00:24:12.300 --> 00:24:14.580
and how we need to scale it.

00:24:14.580 --> 00:24:18.030
When it came down to MongoDB
in the previous architecture,

00:24:18.030 --> 00:24:21.420
it was not as
transparent in terms

00:24:21.420 --> 00:24:25.160
of being able to see where we're
at in terms of the scalability.

00:24:25.160 --> 00:24:27.780
There's no direct correlation
between the number of writes

00:24:27.780 --> 00:24:29.460
and the hardware that we have.

00:24:29.460 --> 00:24:32.160
So we had to do a lot of
benchmarking and testing.

00:24:32.160 --> 00:24:34.980
And typically we
would see that we

00:24:34.980 --> 00:24:36.930
needed to scale
through proxy metrics,

00:24:36.930 --> 00:24:39.170
like the response
times of the APIs,

00:24:39.170 --> 00:24:41.250
rather than actually
knowing that we're

00:24:41.250 --> 00:24:44.760
hitting the limits of the writes
that the product can provide.

00:24:44.760 --> 00:24:45.510
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:24:45.510 --> 00:24:47.050
So I'm actually curious--

00:24:47.050 --> 00:24:50.100
you talked about seemingly
all the good stuff.

00:24:50.100 --> 00:24:52.470
Were there any interesting
challenges or issues

00:24:52.470 --> 00:24:54.930
that you ran into moving
over to GCP and BigTable

00:24:54.930 --> 00:24:56.310
and the new architecture?

00:24:56.310 --> 00:24:57.450
DAVID MYTTON: Yes.

00:24:57.450 --> 00:25:00.210
There were two
interesting situations

00:25:00.210 --> 00:25:01.860
that we were talking about.

00:25:01.860 --> 00:25:06.990
So the first time that we
started the test workload

00:25:06.990 --> 00:25:11.160
onto BigTable, we did
some tests in MongoDB

00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:12.630
and looked at the
number of metrics

00:25:12.630 --> 00:25:15.930
that we were writing per
second, and then simulated

00:25:15.930 --> 00:25:21.370
some test traffic into BigTable
to see if that actually--

00:25:21.370 --> 00:25:22.800
the theory actually played out.

00:25:22.800 --> 00:25:26.610
And we saw that we were
doing hundreds of thousands

00:25:26.610 --> 00:25:29.490
of writes per second more
than we actually predicted.

00:25:29.490 --> 00:25:32.070
And that completely blew
away our cost calculations

00:25:32.070 --> 00:25:36.600
because you get 10,000
writes per second per node,

00:25:36.600 --> 00:25:38.130
and you just pay
per node, and it

00:25:38.130 --> 00:25:40.740
looked like we were going
to go into 200 or 300 nodes

00:25:40.740 --> 00:25:43.260
on Bigtable, which would
have been cool to say,

00:25:43.260 --> 00:25:46.290
and was fun discussing
with your engineers.

00:25:46.290 --> 00:25:50.370
But then we discovered
that OpenTSDB shipped

00:25:50.370 --> 00:25:52.830
with an outdated driver.

00:25:52.830 --> 00:25:59.310
And it was released before
you released a new batch API.

00:25:59.310 --> 00:26:01.140
So your engineer
suggested that we

00:26:01.140 --> 00:26:04.740
upgrade to a test version
of the driver, which we did.

00:26:04.740 --> 00:26:07.020
And in our test
workload, which was

00:26:07.020 --> 00:26:10.020
doing 100,000 writes
per second, as soon

00:26:10.020 --> 00:26:11.670
as we switched over
to the new driver,

00:26:11.670 --> 00:26:14.160
it went down to 3,000
writes per second--

00:26:14.160 --> 00:26:15.110
MARK MANDEL: Oh, wow.

00:26:15.110 --> 00:26:18.630
DAVID MYTTON: --which is a
pretty significant difference.

00:26:18.630 --> 00:26:22.020
It was a big relief that
our actual calculations

00:26:22.020 --> 00:26:25.950
were correct and our original
cost projections were correct.

00:26:25.950 --> 00:26:28.650
Then the second lesson
that we learned from that

00:26:28.650 --> 00:26:32.160
was to do with the keys,
and this is really important

00:26:32.160 --> 00:26:35.370
when you're querying the data.

00:26:35.370 --> 00:26:38.650
So the original key was
based on an account ID,

00:26:38.650 --> 00:26:40.800
say every account
within server density

00:26:40.800 --> 00:26:45.090
gets a unique ID, and
then also the metric name.

00:26:45.090 --> 00:26:49.830
And the more items that we
have in an account, the more

00:26:49.830 --> 00:26:51.810
data that had to be queried.

00:26:51.810 --> 00:26:54.360
And by item I mean
like, a server.

00:26:54.360 --> 00:26:56.380
So if we were looking
for load average,

00:26:56.380 --> 00:26:58.560
for example, then
the original key

00:26:58.560 --> 00:27:02.190
was to look at the account ID
and then look at load average.

00:27:02.190 --> 00:27:05.010
But that would query
across every single server

00:27:05.010 --> 00:27:07.140
within the account.

00:27:07.140 --> 00:27:09.930
So in our performance
testing, that quickly

00:27:09.930 --> 00:27:13.980
revealed that we'd made a
mistake with the key query

00:27:13.980 --> 00:27:16.320
design, and we needed
to be much more

00:27:16.320 --> 00:27:19.289
granular, because for our very
small accounts, it was fine.

00:27:19.289 --> 00:27:21.330
But for our accounts with
thousands and thousands

00:27:21.330 --> 00:27:23.550
of servers, the
response time was just

00:27:23.550 --> 00:27:25.409
completely unacceptable.

00:27:25.409 --> 00:27:27.450
So we played around with
a few different options.

00:27:27.450 --> 00:27:29.730
And the obvious one
was just to drop

00:27:29.730 --> 00:27:32.490
in the item ID in the middle.

00:27:32.490 --> 00:27:35.440
So instead of it just
being account ID and then

00:27:35.440 --> 00:27:39.360
a metric name, it became
account ID, item ID,

00:27:39.360 --> 00:27:40.750
and then the metric name.

00:27:40.750 --> 00:27:44.760
And that reduced query
times from 30 to 40 seconds

00:27:44.760 --> 00:27:49.264
in some cases, down to less
than a second in most cases,

00:27:49.264 --> 00:27:51.180
which is much more
acceptable when you're just

00:27:51.180 --> 00:27:51.960
loading a dashboard.

00:27:51.960 --> 00:27:53.126
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, nice.

00:27:53.126 --> 00:27:55.080
Yeah, I guess that
is one of the things

00:27:55.080 --> 00:27:58.020
that I've heard many
times about BigTable.

00:27:58.020 --> 00:28:00.960
BigTable is, after
all, a key value store.

00:28:00.960 --> 00:28:04.620
A very fancy one at it, but
basically a key value store.

00:28:04.620 --> 00:28:06.150
So I guess that
choosing the keys

00:28:06.150 --> 00:28:08.640
is a very, very
important part of it.

00:28:08.640 --> 00:28:12.709
Is there any other aspects
of any best practices

00:28:12.709 --> 00:28:13.500
that you may share?

00:28:13.500 --> 00:28:16.219
Something that you
learned, say, hey,

00:28:16.219 --> 00:28:17.760
this is how you
should be doing keys,

00:28:17.760 --> 00:28:19.450
other than what
you just mentioned?

00:28:19.450 --> 00:28:23.940
DAVID MYTTON: I think the key
to designing the key set up

00:28:23.940 --> 00:28:26.040
is to actually test it.

00:28:26.040 --> 00:28:29.960
And we tested with small
data sets to begin with.

00:28:29.960 --> 00:28:33.270
It was only when we then started
testing with the production

00:28:33.270 --> 00:28:36.240
simulation that we discovered
this performance problem, which

00:28:36.240 --> 00:28:39.030
in hindsight is pretty
obvious and is something

00:28:39.030 --> 00:28:41.560
that could easily be picked up.

00:28:41.560 --> 00:28:45.390
But it was just a simple
mistake in not having

00:28:45.390 --> 00:28:47.670
a diverse enough testing set.

00:28:47.670 --> 00:28:51.210
And that, I suppose, is
also similar to the problem

00:28:51.210 --> 00:28:53.190
we had with the driver.

00:28:53.190 --> 00:28:55.440
Once you start putting
production workload through it,

00:28:55.440 --> 00:28:57.914
that's when it really
shows up the problems.

00:28:57.914 --> 00:29:00.330
Otherwise, you would just
assume just a couple of thousand

00:29:00.330 --> 00:29:04.050
writes per second as a test
workload is a reasonably

00:29:04.050 --> 00:29:05.460
sized workload for many people.

00:29:05.460 --> 00:29:08.670
But as soon as you start pushing
actual production data through,

00:29:08.670 --> 00:29:10.805
that's when these little
things will show up.

00:29:10.805 --> 00:29:11.940
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:29:11.940 --> 00:29:14.040
Well, we're slowly
running out of time here.

00:29:14.040 --> 00:29:16.619
Is there anything we haven't
asked you or anything

00:29:16.619 --> 00:29:18.660
you want to mention or
plug or anything like that

00:29:18.660 --> 00:29:21.450
before we finish up?

00:29:21.450 --> 00:29:24.090
DAVID MYTTON: I think the only
thing would be the HumanOps

00:29:24.090 --> 00:29:28.890
community that I started, and
I'm now running, in the US

00:29:28.890 --> 00:29:33.720
and also in Europe, which has
come out of my experiences

00:29:33.720 --> 00:29:35.780
in the early days
of Server Density

00:29:35.780 --> 00:29:39.202
being on call 24 hours a
day for a couple of years,

00:29:39.202 --> 00:29:41.160
but now more recently
talking to our customers.

00:29:41.160 --> 00:29:44.010
And it's a community
and a series

00:29:44.010 --> 00:29:46.949
of meet-ups where we get
companies and individuals

00:29:46.949 --> 00:29:48.490
to come along and
talk about how they

00:29:48.490 --> 00:29:51.540
are running the human
side of their operations.

00:29:51.540 --> 00:29:54.810
We tend to focus on all the
cool shiny tools and systems

00:29:54.810 --> 00:29:57.870
that we have in place, but
don't pay enough attention

00:29:57.870 --> 00:30:00.630
to the humans that are running
the systems in the sense

00:30:00.630 --> 00:30:02.820
of fatigue, getting
too many alerts,

00:30:02.820 --> 00:30:05.910
all the stress of being on call,
and how different companies

00:30:05.910 --> 00:30:06.910
deal with that.

00:30:06.910 --> 00:30:09.660
So we've run an event
in San Francisco.

00:30:09.660 --> 00:30:12.469
We're doing one in
April in New York.

00:30:12.469 --> 00:30:14.010
And we're also
running one in London.

00:30:14.010 --> 00:30:16.024
So just have a look
on HumanOps.com.

00:30:16.024 --> 00:30:16.940
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:30:16.940 --> 00:30:19.050
And since we are
talking about events,

00:30:19.050 --> 00:30:22.157
it seems like we'll
see you at Cloud Next.

00:30:22.157 --> 00:30:23.490
DAVID MYTTON: Yes, that's right.

00:30:23.490 --> 00:30:26.715
On 8th of March, I'm going to
be speaking about this, about

00:30:26.715 --> 00:30:27.730
the migration.

00:30:27.730 --> 00:30:30.170
I've got some cool
architecture diagrams

00:30:30.170 --> 00:30:33.690
so you can visualize what I've
just been talking through.

00:30:33.690 --> 00:30:35.470
And we'll see.

00:30:35.470 --> 00:30:37.770
I've also got the
graph of that change

00:30:37.770 --> 00:30:39.940
to the driver with
that massive drop

00:30:39.940 --> 00:30:42.317
from 100,000 down to
3,000 writes per second.

00:30:42.317 --> 00:30:43.650
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Sounds awesome.

00:30:43.650 --> 00:30:44.070
MARK MANDEL: Great.

00:30:44.070 --> 00:30:46.100
Well, thank you so much for
joining us today, David.

00:30:46.100 --> 00:30:47.940
That was a super
interesting conversation.

00:30:47.940 --> 00:30:48.390
DAVID MYTTON: Excellent.

00:30:48.390 --> 00:30:49.170
Thank you.

00:30:49.170 --> 00:30:51.270
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So thanks
again to David for such

00:30:51.270 --> 00:30:52.830
an interesting interview.

00:30:52.830 --> 00:30:56.670
It was pretty enlightening to
