00:32:22.948 --> 00:32:26.770
Well, why don't we move on
to our question of the week.

00:32:26.770 --> 00:32:28.380
Today's question of
the week, we want

00:32:28.380 --> 00:32:30.410
to talk about
[? queuing ?] times.

00:32:30.410 --> 00:32:35.310
So you're building your
next amazing application

00:32:35.310 --> 00:32:37.410
and you say to
yourself, which regions

00:32:37.410 --> 00:32:39.540
should I put this in for
Google Cloud Platform?

00:32:39.540 --> 00:32:42.850
Which ones are going to
give me the lowest latency?

00:32:42.850 --> 00:32:45.210
So is there an easy way
to be able to find out

00:32:45.210 --> 00:32:46.620
what my latencies could be?

00:32:46.620 --> 00:32:48.536
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Not
only there's an easy way,

00:32:48.536 --> 00:32:51.450
but it has an amazing name.

00:32:51.450 --> 00:32:52.920
It is GCPing.

00:32:52.920 --> 00:32:56.160
So like GCP, but with
I-N-G at the end, which

00:32:56.160 --> 00:32:59.400
also is ping, so GCPing.com.

00:32:59.400 --> 00:33:02.190
And it is a very simple service.

00:33:02.190 --> 00:33:07.190
It is created by someone working
at Google, Jason [? Hull. ?]

00:33:07.190 --> 00:33:09.690
He just made this available,
it's a very simple service.

00:33:09.690 --> 00:33:13.020
It has one f1-micro instance
running every single one

00:33:13.020 --> 00:33:17.580
of our regions, and basically
just shows you the average ping

00:33:17.580 --> 00:33:20.340
time from your laptop--

00:33:20.340 --> 00:33:22.880
or whatever you're
using, maybe your phone--

00:33:22.880 --> 00:33:24.830
to all of those instances.

00:33:24.830 --> 00:33:25.740
And it's pretty cool.

00:33:25.740 --> 00:33:27.180
For instance, right
now, for me, I

00:33:27.180 --> 00:33:32.850
can see that from San Francisco,
the shortest ping is US west

00:33:32.850 --> 00:33:35.460
1, which is not surprising.

00:33:35.460 --> 00:33:39.720
But I was actually surprised
that Europe west 1 is slightly

00:33:39.720 --> 00:33:42.780
longer than Asia east
1, which kind of makes

00:33:42.780 --> 00:33:45.540
sense because Asia east
is just the ocean, so we

00:33:45.540 --> 00:33:46.950
have a cable going through.

00:33:46.950 --> 00:33:50.466
But US west 1, you need to
go through the United States

00:33:50.466 --> 00:33:51.590
and then through the ocean.

00:33:51.590 --> 00:33:51.780
MARK MANDEL: Oh, yeah.

00:33:51.780 --> 00:33:53.050
There's like mountains
and stuff in the way.

00:33:53.050 --> 00:33:54.310
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, so
it kind of makes sense.

00:33:54.310 --> 00:33:56.760
That if you didn't think
about it, even flying,

00:33:56.760 --> 00:34:02.356
actually, I'd say that Paris
is shorter than Hong Kong.

00:34:02.356 --> 00:34:03.720
Apparently that doesn't count.

00:34:03.720 --> 00:34:04.970
MARK MANDEL: It doesn't count.

00:34:04.970 --> 00:34:05.767
We have cables.

00:34:05.767 --> 00:34:07.350
But one thing I
really like about this

00:34:07.350 --> 00:34:09.639
is there's a global
world in there.

00:34:09.639 --> 00:34:12.540
So what he does is he
set up a global HTTP load

00:34:12.540 --> 00:34:15.659
balancer, which will then route
the request to the nearest

00:34:15.659 --> 00:34:16.800
healthy instance.

00:34:16.800 --> 00:34:20.929
So what's neat about that is you
can actually see that for me,

00:34:20.929 --> 00:34:23.010
again, same as San
Francisco, US west 1,

00:34:23.010 --> 00:34:25.730
I get about 28 milliseconds.

00:34:25.730 --> 00:34:27.989
And your mileage may
vary for obvious reasons.

00:34:27.989 --> 00:34:29.635
But then my global
is 31 milliseconds.

00:34:29.635 --> 00:34:32.010
So there's a small difference
as it goes through the load

00:34:32.010 --> 00:34:34.301
balance every few milliseconds,
but you can pretty much

00:34:34.301 --> 00:34:36.237
determine where it's
actually routed you

00:34:36.237 --> 00:34:38.070
to the correct place
that is closest to you.

00:34:38.070 --> 00:34:39.653
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, it is very cool

00:34:39.653 --> 00:34:42.909
because you can imagine that
if you want your instance

00:34:42.909 --> 00:34:45.449
to be accessible
from-- your application

00:34:45.449 --> 00:34:49.080
to be accessible and fast
everywhere in the world, yeah,

00:34:49.080 --> 00:34:51.720
you need to have a global
HTTP load balancer.

00:34:51.720 --> 00:34:52.969
That is the answer, basically.

00:34:52.969 --> 00:34:55.760
But if you are depending
on a specific region,

00:34:55.760 --> 00:34:58.970
yeah, GCPing.com and you can see
which one is the closest one.

00:34:58.970 --> 00:34:59.850
